@startuml
title Bedside station v1 state machine

[*] --> Booting
Booting --> idle : WiFi, NTP and audio ready\nno enabled alarm
Booting --> armed : valid enabled alarm loaded
Booting --> fault : blocking fault

idle --> armed : enabled alarm loaded
idle --> fault : time/config/audio fault

armed --> idle : alarm disabled
armed --> ringing : local alarm time reached\nbracelet ready
armed --> idle : 10-minute activity window expired\nor revision already completed
armed --> fault : no valid time/config\nbracelet missing/low/fault

ringing --> validating_activity : valid v6 ESP-NOW movement event <= 10 s old\nXSMT LOW for remaining 10 s interval
ringing --> stopped : 10-minute activity window ended
ringing --> ringing : bracelet timeout > 3 s\npublish bracelet_missing, keep audio playing
ringing --> fault : audio fault

validating_activity --> ringing : mute expires, vibration is acknowledged,\n3 s warning plus 500 ms guard expires
validating_activity --> ringing : vibration acknowledgement missing for 3 s\nresume conservatively
validating_activity --> validating_activity : bracelet timeout > 3 s\npublish bracelet_missing, honor existing deadline
validating_activity --> stopped : 10-minute activity window ended
validating_activity --> fault : audio fault

stopped --> idle : alarm cycle complete

fault --> idle : problem fixed\nno enabled alarm
fault --> armed : problem fixed\nvalid enabled alarm

note right of ringing
Supabase track playback failure does not stop alarm.
The station switches to generated local fallback audio.
Audio starts at the configured alarm time, while
bracelet vibration remains disabled for 15 seconds.
After that delay, vibration requests use the existing flow.
Periodic config refresh does not leave ringing
or validating_activity.
Network status refreshes are deferred while
streaming remote audio to avoid underruns.
The station uses an enlarged audio buffer and
prioritizes audio.loop() while remote audio runs.
Transition status writes are also deferred, and
the remote stream is primed before the station
requests bracelet vibration.
end note

note bottom
There is no normal stop or snooze transition.
Bracelet energy only mutes audio during the
fixed 10-minute activity window by toggling XSMT.
Movement processing and mute timing stay active during
the initial 15-second vibration-free interval.
The bracelet retransmits its newest qualifying event
for up to 10 seconds. The station subtracts event age
from the original 10-second mute and ignores duplicates.
Packet loss does not cancel an already confirmed mute.
Audio resumes after a matching vibration acknowledgement,
or conservatively after a 3-second acknowledgement timeout.
The stream or fallback audio keeps running until
the station marks the alarm complete when that
window ends.
end note

@enduml
